SECTION I.
Introduction

Whether the pilot can fly the aircraft safely and efficiently is directly affected by the design of the aircraft information display. The survey analyzing the reasons of the human errors of the pilot shows that: during the aircraft landing process, more than 50% of the flight malignant accidents occur at the moment when the pilot looks down at the instrument and looks up at the surrounding environment, because it leads to the attention shift [1] . Head-up display (HUD) which presents the information and image through the windshield in front of the pilot enables the pilot observe the surrounding environment and the displayed information at the same time, so that the risk of the accident caused by the information loss during the aircraft landing process can be reduced.

As a newly developed primary flight display of the civil aircraft, HUD draws a great attention of the domestic and international airlines, and China is going to apply the HUD technology to C919 aircraft which is being developed. One of the main problems existing in the design of the HUD information display is the humanity of the identifier design, such as the non-standardization of the identifier, limited field of view, as well as failure of error detection and less warning display [2] . Therefore, it is a very critical issue to design the HUD's information display format appropriately, thus to help the pilot identify the targets and understand the external environment efficiently.

In the early 1990s, the U.S. Air Force Instrument Flight Center was assigned to develop a set of standardized HUD symbols together with the Amstrong Laboratory Human System Center and Wright Laboratory Aviation Center. Their purpose was to enhance the flight efficiency and reduce the human errors during the flight [2] . By now, the Boeing and Airbus have formed a relatively complete design system of the HUD information display, and have successfully applied them to the HUD design of A380 and B787.

Peter Chudy and Karol Rydlo from Brno University of Technology presented an idea of applying the curved belt scale to the synthetic vision display on the aircraft [3] . D.S. Lee et al. studied the effect of character size under ambient illuminances and light sources on legibility of electronic paper displays [4] . Xiaochao Guo et al. compared different HUD information display formats of airspeed, height, and heading which affected pilot's cognitive performance [5] . Yuelong Zhu conducted the study on the ergonomics issues of belt scale display in the aircraft cockpit [6] . Kazuhiro Fujikake and Satoshi Hasegawa performed an experiment about the readability of character size on a car navigation system [7] . Lei Zhang studied the information coding including color, position, and language on a flight simulator and measured the subjects' correct rate, reaction time, and eye movement data as the performance evaluation indexes [8] . During Yingwei Zhou's experiment, three character types, three eye distances from the display interface, five character sizes were adopted to verify the humanity of coding design for human-machine display interface [9] .

On the basis of the above literature analysis, the present study carries out the ergonomic design and evaluation of the visual coding for aircraft HUD. Making use of the powerful OpenGL animation processing capability and VS2008 programming platform, the aircraft HUD layout and display simulation model is developed, and then the ergonomics experiment and evaluation method based on the subjects' behavior performance test is designed. The displayed content of the simulation model included the bar belt scale or the curved belt scale, belt scale display combined with a numeric box or not, two stroke widths, three color codes of the error messages, as well as four different aircraft symbols.
SECTION II.
Development of Aircraft HUD Layout and Information Display Simulation Model

The present study adopts OpenGL and VS2008 for programming to simulate the HUD information display during the flight, so that the recognition and restoration tasks of the abnormal messages can be achieved [10] .
A. HUD layout and information interaction simulation
1) HUD layout and information display

The HUD layout refers to the HUD of A380 and B787, and the main display contents are airspeed, height and attitude, while the attitude includes roll angle, pitch angle and course angle. The information layout adopts the T-shaped layout which is widely accepted, where the airspeed is on the left, the height is on the right, and the roll angle, pitch angle and course angle are presented in the middle of the interface from the top to the bottom. Moreover, the background of the HUD interface is set as the blue sky and the color of the normal information is the widely used green.
2) Animated simulation

To as close as possible to simulate the real HUD, the belt scale needs to achieve a smooth free move with the numbers in the numeric box changing synchronously. The animated simulation of the HUD interface should realize the animation effects according to the motion law of the belt scale.
3) Human-machine interaction

Human-machine interaction is the basis of the ergonomics experiment. It can mainly implement the following functions: put the random appearance of the abnormal messages into practice, get the CPU time, and record the subject's key-press response correct rate and the reaction time which can been chosen as the performance evaluation indexes.
B. The establishment of the Simulation model

The establishment of the simulation model includes the following steps. (1) The establishment of the window: create a new window for the display of the belt scale and data. (2) The drawing of the geometric graphics and the loading of the background picture: the geometric graphics include the belt scale, the rectangular box and the aircraft symbol while the background picture is designed as the blue sky. (3) Geometric coordinate transformation: in order to bring out the 3D visual effects needed by the simulation model, coordinate transformation should be used to fulfill the movement of the shapes and objects. (4) The loading of the flight data: load the airspeed and height data which should be displayed in real time. (5) Real time animation and double buffering mechanism: these two functions guarantee the smooth animation effects of the HUD interface. (6) The achievement of human-machine interaction: it's the basis of the experiment which has three main problems to be considered, as mentioned in A.
C. The overall effect of simulation

As shown in Fig. 1 , the four aircraft symbols used in the experiment are designed referring to the relevant aircraft symbols of GJB300–87, A380, and B787. They are located in the middle of the HUD interface to help the pilot identify the roll and pitch information.

The contrast of the simulation results for the belt scale, numeric box, stroke width, and color in this experiment are shown as Fig. 2(a) , Fig. 2(b) , Fig. 2(c) , and Fig. 2(d) , among which, a and b are located in both sides of the HUD interface to help the pilot identify the airspeed and height information, while c and d are applied to all of the information displayed on the interface and will affect the subject's identification performance of all the information.

Fig. 3 and Fig. 4 are the overall interfaces of the HUD layout and information display simulation. There are five displayed information including airspeed, height, roll angle, pitch angle and course angle, while the belt scale and color are different between these two interfaces.
