SECTION III.
Experiment Design
A. Experimental apparatus and interface

The experiment is conducted on an IBM PC with 3.30 GHz CPU and 1440×600 display pixel resolution. The subjects use the keyboard to finish the human-machine interaction in normal operation position. Similar as Fig. 3 and Fig. 4 , there are twenty eight experiment interfaces designed in all, twenty four of them consider the interaction effects of belt scale, numeric box, stroke width and color, and the other four interfaces consider the single factor of aircraft symbols.
B. Experiment tasks

The experiment lasted for eight minutes. Abnormal message corresponding to one of the five types of flight information appears randomly for two seconds and then disappears. The subjects need to recognize, judge and response to the abnormal message as fast and accurately as possible. The computer records the operation performance (including correct detection, wrong detection and failure detection) as well as the reaction time by itself.
C. Subjects

Eighteen flight cadets (ranging from 21 to 31 years, mean age 23.9 years) from Beihang University participated in the present study. Their vision or corrected visual acuity is normal, and written informed consent was obtained prior to the study.
D. Experiment method

Eighteen subjects are divided into three groups randomly in the experiment. The factor of color is chosen as the between-subj ect variable, while the belt scale, numeric box, and stroke width are chosen as the within-subject variables. The eight interfaces' order in every group is counterbalanced across the subjects to eliminate the practice effects. In addition, six subjects chosen from the eighteen randomly take the comparative experiment of the four kinds of the aircraft symbols.
SECTION IV.
Experiment Result and Analysis
A. Experiment result and data processing

The collection and the arrangement of the experiment data are completed automatically during the experiment. Further data processing is conducted in the IBM SPSS Statistics data editor. The results of the descriptive statistics and multi-factor variance analysis which is the most suitable variance analysis for the data are shown in Tab. 1 and Tab. 2 .

For the reaction time, the multi-factor variance analysis results show that on the 0.05 significant level, the main effect of the belt scale is significant ( P < 0.001 ) , exhibiting as the reaction time of the bar belt scale is less than the curved one; the main effect of the numeric box is significant ( P = 0.005 ) , exhibiting as the reaction time of the belt with the numeric box is less than the one without it; the main effect of the stroke width is significant ( P < 0.001 ) , exhibiting as the reaction time of the thick stroke width is less than the thin one; the main effect of the color is significant ( P < 0.001 ) , exhibiting as the reaction time of the red is less than yellow, and the yellow is less than the green; the main effect of the aircraft symbol is not significant ( P = 0.915 > 0.05 ) .

For the correct rate, the multi-factor variance analysis results show that on the 0.05 significant level, the main effect of the belt scale is significant ( P < 0.001 ) , manifesting as the correct rate of bar belt scale is higher than the curved one; the main effect of the numeric box is not that significant ( P = 0.084 ) , manifesting as the correct rate of the belt with the numeric box is higher than the one without it; the main effect of the stroke width is significant ( P < 0.001 ) , manifesting as the correct rate of the thick stroke width is higher than the thin one; the main effect of the color is significant ( P < 0.001 ) , and the correct rate of the red is higher than the yellow, and the yellow is higher than the green; the main effect of the aircraft symbol is not significant ( P = 0.862 > 0.05 ) .

According to the above results, the post hoc multiple comparisons of the color need to be performed. As shown in Tab. 3 , for the correct rate, the differences between green and yellow, green and red, as well as yellow and red is significant ( P < 0.05 ) ; for the reaction time, the differences between green and yellow, green and red is significant ( P < 0.051 ) , however, the difference between yellow and red is not significant ( P > 0.05 ) .
